    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/ncomms2704",
    "abstract": "Climate models are seen by many to be unverifiable. However, near-term climate predictions up to 10 years into the future carried out recently with these models can be rigorously verified against observations. Near-term climate prediction is a new information tool for the climate adaptation and service communities, which often make decisions on near-term time scales, and for which the most basic information is unfortunately very scarce. The Fifth Coupled Model Intercomparison Project set of co-ordinated climate-model experiments includes a set of near-term predictions in which several modelling groups participated and whose forecast quality we illustrate here. We show that climate forecast systems have skill in predicting the Earth's temperature at regional scales over the past 50 years and illustrate the trustworthiness of their predictions. Most of the skill can be attributed to changes in atmospheric composition, but also partly to the initialization of the predictions.",
    "field": "Near-term climate prediction",
    "problem": "Existing climate projections provide scarce and unverified information for near-term regional forecasting because they neglect the initial state of the climate system, failing to capture predictable internal climate variability crucial for adaptation decisions on decadal timescales.",
    "method": "This paper assesses the skill of initialized near-term climate predictions by analyzing a multi-model ensemble of hindcasts, comparing them with non-initialized projections and observations to show that initializing forecast systems with observed data improves regional temperature predictions up to a decade ahead.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the skill and trustworthiness of initialized near-term regional climate predictions by verifying multi-model hindcasts from the CMIP5 project against observations over the past 50 years to determine the added value of initialization over non-initialized climate projections.",
